+# Design: Self-initiating, adaptive Forge skill flow
+
+**Feature:** forge-adaptive-skills
+**Date:** 2026-07-16
+**Status:** design (for team-lead review — NOT for implementation)
+**Kernel issue:** 2b71e189-43b1-4e17-89ba-fabd9175c829 (parent 64d6c458)
+**Reconciles with:** a0776e61 (skill composition/relationships), 9661e78a (ecosystem cross-check), 2ef2bca5 (skill-router)
+
+---
+
+## Problem
+
+The owner praised superpowers' skill flow in the CuraPod session and wants Forge's **own**
+skills to match its quality. Four properties made it "brilliant":
+
+1. **Self-initiation** — the right skill announces itself before any response.
+2. **Orient-first** — it understands the actual repo before opining.
+3. **Structured elicitation** — one question at a time, refuses to over-simplify, commits a design doc.
+4. **Explicit terminal state + declared next skill** — one and only one next skill, named imperatively.
+
+Forge's stage skills are strong on gates but express these four properties weakly or implicitly.
+This design makes them a **shared, explicit convention** layered onto the existing 6-stage
+workflow — an enhancement, not a replacement.
+
+Note: the superpowers `brainstorming` skill is already usable inside Forge repos today. This work
+is about Forge's own `/plan → /dev → …` skills adopting the same flow quality.
+
+---
+
+## 1. How superpowers' skill flow actually works (grounded in real files)
+
+Inspected on this machine at
+`~/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/6.1.1/skills/`.
+
+### 1a. Self-initiation — the `using-superpowers` bootstrap
+`skills/using-superpowers/SKILL.md` is the engine. It is loaded at session start (the plugin's
+`CLAUDE.md` "acceptance test" requires that sending "Let's make a react todo list" auto-triggers
+`brainstorming` *before any code*). Its mechanics:
+
+- `<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>` "**If you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply … you
+  ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill**" (L10–16).
+- **The Rule** (L18–24): "Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action —
+  including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files." Then: "announce
+  'Using [skill] to [purpose]' and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo
+  per item."
+- A **Red Flags** rationalization table (L33–50): every "let me just check files first / this is a
+  simple question / I remember this skill" thought is paired with the reality that refutes it.
+- **Skill Priority** (L26–31): process skills (brainstorming, systematic-debugging) run first and
+  set the approach; implementation skills carry it out.
+
+Self-initiation is therefore **two mechanisms working together**: (i) a bootstrap skill that
+forbids acting before invoking, and (ii) skill **descriptions that carry the trigger**, because the
+model only sees descriptions until invocation. `brainstorming`'s description *is* the trigger:
+"**You MUST use this before any creative work** - creating features, building components …"
+(`brainstorming/SKILL.md` L3).
+
+### 1b. Orient-first
+`brainstorming/SKILL.md` checklist item 1 (L24) is "**Explore project context** — check files,
+docs, recent commits", and Process L67 "Check out the current project state first". The issue
+quotes the CuraPod session's orient line (L182): "First, let me orient — understand how your
+knowledge base is actually laid out today before I offer opinions." Orientation precedes opinion.
+
+### 1c. Structured elicitation (and refusal to over-simplify)
+- **Anti-Pattern: "This Is Too Simple To Need A Design"** (L16–18) + a `<HARD-GATE>` (L12–14)
+  forbidding any implementation action until a design is presented and approved — "regardless of
+  perceived simplicity."
+- **One question at a time**, multiple-choice preferred, cover purpose/constraints/success criteria
+  (L70–73, L135–140).
+- **Propose 2–3 approaches** with a recommendation (L76–80).
+- **Present the design in sections scaled to complexity, approval after each** (L82–87).
+- Terminates in a **committed design doc** with a self-review pass (placeholder/consistency/scope/
+  ambiguity scan, L111–119) and a **user review gate** (L121–126).
+
+### 1d. Explicit terminal state + declared next skill (chaining)
+This is the signature move. Each skill declares exactly one successor:
+
+- `brainstorming` L61: "**The terminal state is invoking writing-plans.** Do NOT invoke
+  frontend-design, mcp-builder, or any other implementation skill. **The ONLY skill you invoke
+  after brainstorming is writing-plans.**" It is restated at L128–131.
+- The chain is also **machine-readable**: a `dot` **Process Flow** digraph whose terminal node is a
+  `doublecircle` labelled "Invoke writing-plans skill" (L36–59).
+- `writing-plans/SKILL.md` continues the chain: every plan starts with a header naming the
+  "**REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: superpowers:subagent-driven-development**" (L61), and the Execution Handoff
+  (L157–174) offers exactly two successors, each with a **REQUIRED SUB-SKILL** line. So
+  brainstorming → writing-plans → subagent-driven-development / executing-plans, each node naming
+  its own required next node.
+
+### 1e. Adaptive to repo conventions
+Defaults are stated but yield to the repo: `brainstorming` L107 "(User preferences for spec
+location override this default)" and `writing-plans` L19 "(User preferences for plan location
+override this default)". The issue cites L383 of the session, where the skill deferred to the
+repo's own `07-Planning/` folder over its default. Adaptivity = "here is my default, but your repo
+wins."
+
+---
+
+## 2. How Forge's current skills differ
+
+Read: `skills/plan/SKILL.md`, `skills/dev/SKILL.md`, `skills/status/SKILL.md`, `AGENTS.md`.
+
+| Property | Superpowers | Forge today | Gap |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| **Self-initiation** | `using-superpowers` bootstrap enforces "invoke before any response" + 1% rule + Red Flags | Rich trigger-carrying descriptions exist (e.g. `plan` desc lists explicit triggers), but **no bootstrap skill** forces "invoke the matching stage before acting." Relies on the harness matching a description. | **Biggest gap.** No session-start enforcer; no anti-rationalization table. |
+| **Orient-first** | brainstorming *opens* by exploring context and states findings before opining | `plan` Step 1 "Explore project context" exists, but it sits **after** ~130 lines of worktree gate + conflict-detect + pr-coordinator + team-verify machinery. `/status` is a dedicated orient skill but is a separate invocation, not an in-skill opening. | Orientation is present but **buried**; skills don't lead by stating what they found. |
+| **Structured elicitation** | one-question-at-a-time + 2–3 approaches + anti-simplification HARD-GATE | `plan` Phase 1 Step 2 "Ask clarifying questions — one at a time" and Step 3 "Propose approaches" **match well**. | Good — but **no anti-simplification gate**; nothing stops "this is too small to plan." |
+| **Terminal state + declared next skill** | imperative "the ONLY skill you invoke next is X" + doublecircle digraph node | `plan` has strong exit **HARD-GATEs** and an "Integration with Workflow" **table/ladder**, but the successor is expressed **passively** (a table row), not "the ONE next skill is `/dev`." No process-flow digraph. | Chaining exists as documentation, not as an **imperative single-successor declaration**. |
+| **Adaptive to repo** | "user preferences override this default location" idiom | Some adaptivity: D34 (external planner artifacts satisfy `/dev` entry), `.forge/config.yaml` composition, gates. | No lightweight "your repo's convention overrides my default" idiom in the skill body. |
+
+**Summary:** Forge is *stronger* than superpowers on validation gates and kernel-native state, but
+*weaker* on the four flow properties: it has no self-initiation enforcer, buries orientation behind
+machinery, lacks an anti-simplification stop, and declares successors passively.
+
+---
+
+## 3. The adaptive-flow design: the Forge Skill Flow Contract
+
+Introduce one convention — the **Skill Flow Contract** — that every Forge stage skill adopts. Four
+required elements + one cross-cutting rule. It is expressed as a small, fixed **section template**
+so it reads identically across skills (the property the owner liked: predictable, self-announcing).
+
+### Element A — Self-initiation (the `activation` bootstrap skill)
+Create a Forge analog of `using-superpowers`: a skill named **`activation`** (candidate; short,
+per the "simple command names" convention) loaded at session start. It contains:
+
+- **The Rule:** invoke the matching Forge stage/utility skill *before any response or action* —
+  including clarifying questions and file exploration.
+- **1%-style trigger:** if there is a real chance a Forge skill applies, invoke it; announce
+  "Using /<skill> to <purpose>" and, if it has a checklist, file the todos.
+- **A Forge Red-Flags table:** "let me just check `forge ready` first", "this is a one-line fix",
+  "I'll plan after I look at the code" → each refuted.
+- **Router seam:** `activation` is the front door for skill-router **2ef2bca5** — it maps the
+  request to a stage (new/unscoped → `/plan`; existing task list → `/dev`; "where am I" →
+  `/status`; bug/failure → `systematic-debugging` then a stage). This is where the router's
+  classification logic is surfaced to the model.
+- Descriptions stay trigger-carrying (already true for `plan`/`dev`/`status`); `activation` only
+  guarantees the model *consults* them first.
+
+Note the boundary vs superpowers: `using-superpowers` says process-skills-first, then
+implementation. Forge's process skill is `/plan` (which already *embeds* brainstorming-style
+elicitation), so `activation` routes "let's build X" → `/plan`, not to a separate brainstorm.
+
+### Element B — Orient-first header
+Every stage skill opens with a fixed `## Orient (before anything else)` block that runs **before**
+gates and elicitation and **states its findings before opining**:
+
+```
+## Orient (before anything else)
+1. forge recap <id>        # resume the issue's own record, if one exists
+2. bash scripts/smart-status.sh   # or /status — stage, active work, ranked issues
+3. Read the touched module + recent commits for this area
+4. STATE what you found in one paragraph BEFORE offering any opinion or asking Q1.
+   ("Here's how <area> is laid out today: …")
+```
+
+Heavy machinery (conflict-detect, pr-coordinator, team-verify, dep-guard) moves **below** Orient
+and is explicitly marked *advisory / soft-block*, so orientation is the first thing the user sees —
+fixing the "buried orientation" gap without deleting the checks.
+
+### Element C — Structured elicitation block
+A shared elicitation sub-section, reused verbatim by any skill that gathers intent (primarily
+`/plan` Phase 1):
+
+- **One question per message**, multiple-choice preferred, cover purpose / constraints / success
+  criteria / edge cases / real technical choices.
+- **Anti-simplification note** (Forge analog of superpowers' anti-pattern): *"No change is 'too
+  small to scope.' The design can be two sentences, but intent + success criteria + out-of-scope
+  must be captured and confirmed before code."* Scaled for Forge: for **Simple/Hotfix/Docs**
+  change-classes (per AGENTS.md) this collapses to a one-line intent confirmation rather than full
+  Q&A — adaptive, not bureaucratic.
+- **Propose 2–3 approaches** with a recommendation; present the design in sections scaled to
+  complexity, confirm each.
+
+### Element D — Terminal state + declared next skill
+Every skill ends with a fixed `## Terminal state → next skill` block that:
+
+- Names **exactly one** imperative successor: e.g. `plan` → *"The one skill you invoke next is
+  `/dev`. Do not jump to `/ship` or hand-implement."* `dev` → `/validate`; `validate` → `/ship`;
+  `ship` → `/review`; `review` → `/verify` (or pre-merge gate); `verify` → done/`/status`.
+- Carries a **machine-readable successor** for skill-router **2ef2bca5** and the
+  composition/relationships graph **a0776e61**: a one-line front-matter-style key
+  (`next-skill: dev`, `terminal: false`) and/or a small `dot` process-flow digraph whose terminal
+  node is the successor — the same doublecircle device superpowers uses. This makes the chain
+  **queryable** (`forge options why`, router graph) instead of prose-only.
+- Reuses Forge's existing kernel-native stage-transition comment (Stage:/Summary:/Decisions:/
+  Artifacts:/Next:) as the **evidence** that the terminal state was reached — Forge is *stronger*
+  here than superpowers, so we keep it and just add the imperative single-successor line above it.
+
+### Cross-cutting — Adaptive to repo conventions
+Each default location or policy in a skill gets the superpowers idiom: *"Default: `docs/work/…`.
+`.forge/config.yaml` or an existing repo convention overrides this default."* Skills read
+`.forge/config.yaml` for the active composition (already the v3 direction) and honor an existing
+docs/plans folder over their default — the L383 behavior the owner called out.
+
+### The contract, in one picture
+```
+activation (bootstrap, self-initiating)         [Element A]
+   │  routes request → stage skill
+   ▼
+┌─────────────── every stage skill ───────────────┐
+│ ## Orient (state findings first)      [Element B]│
+│ ## Elicit (1 Q/msg, no-too-simple)    [Element C]│  ← only intent-gathering skills
+│ ## <stage work + existing HARD-GATEs>            │
+│ ## Terminal state → next skill        [Element D]│
+│    next-skill: <one successor> + digraph         │
+└──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
+   │  imperative single successor
+   ▼  (kernel-native Stage: transition comment = evidence)
+ next stage skill …
+```
+
+---
+
+## 4. Composition with the existing 6-stage workflow (enhancement, not replacement)
+
+The 6 stages (`/plan → /dev → /validate → /ship → /review → /verify`, plus embedded pre-merge gate,
+plus `/status` + `/shepherd` utilities) and all their HARD-GATEs **stay exactly as they are**. The
+Contract is **additive**:
+
+- **A (self-initiation)** is genuinely new: the `activation` bootstrap + router front door. It does
+  not change any stage — it ensures the correct stage *starts itself*.
+- **B (orient-first)** *reorders* content already inside `/plan` (and adds a 3-line opener to the
+  other stages), moving the existing "Explore project context" ahead of the advisory machinery.
+  Nothing is deleted.
+- **C (elicitation)** is mostly a *relabel* of `/plan` Phase 1 into the shared block + the new
+  anti-simplification note, with the Simple/Hotfix/Docs collapse so it stays proportional.
+- **D (terminal + next skill)** *upgrades* the existing "Integration with Workflow" ladder and exit
+  HARD-GATEs from passive tables to one imperative successor line + a machine-readable
+  `next-skill`/digraph. The kernel-native Stage: transition comments already in every skill become
+  the terminal-state evidence.
+- **Adaptive** hooks into `.forge/config.yaml` composition (already v3) — the Contract is one
+  composition over runtime building blocks, consistent with AGENTS.md's "not a product-wide
+  mandatory ladder."
+
+**Reconciliation with the referenced epics:**
+- **2ef2bca5 skill-router** — `activation` *is* the router's user-facing front door; `next-skill`
+  keys give the router its edges.
+- **a0776e61 skill composition/relationships** — Element D's machine-readable successor turns the
+  implicit ladder into an explicit, queryable relationship graph.
+- **9661e78a ecosystem cross-check** — the Contract is a single template applied uniformly, which
+  is exactly what a cross-check verifies (every skill has Orient / Terminal / next-skill).
+
+**Result:** the owner's four liked properties become guaranteed, uniform, and machine-checkable,
+while Forge keeps its gate/kernel strengths.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Task breakdown for implementation (proposed, for team-lead)
+
+Ordered; foundational first. Each is independently reviewable.
+
+1. **Author the Contract spec** — a short `docs/reference/skill-flow-contract.md` defining the four
+   fixed sections (Orient / Elicit / Terminal→next / adaptive idiom), the `next-skill` +
+   `terminal` metadata keys, and the digraph convention. Single source the other tasks cite.
+2. **Create the `activation` bootstrap skill** — `skills/activation/SKILL.md`: the Rule, 1% trigger,
+   Forge Red-Flags table, router mapping (request → stage). Verify it loads at session start in the
+   Claude mirror. (Coordinate with skill-router 2ef2bca5 owner.)
+3. **Add `next-skill`/`terminal` metadata + imperative Terminal block** to all 6 stage skills +
+   `/status`, `/shepherd`. Upgrade each "Integration with Workflow" table to one imperative
+   successor line; add the small process-flow digraph. (Mechanical, per-skill; Sonnet-suitable.)
+4. **Add the Orient-first header** to each stage skill; move `/plan`'s advisory machinery below it;
+   mark advisory blocks as soft-block. (Care: preserve every existing check.)
+5. **Extract the shared Elicitation block + anti-simplification note**; wire the
+   Simple/Hotfix/Docs proportional collapse to AGENTS.md change-classification.
+6. **Adaptive-convention pass** — add the "config/repo convention overrides this default" idiom to
+   every default path/policy; have skills read `.forge/config.yaml` composition.
+7. **Ecosystem cross-check gate (9661e78a)** — a lint/test asserting every stage skill has Orient,
+   a single `next-skill` (or `terminal: true`), and the Terminal block. Wire into the drift gate.
+8. **Skill-sync + mirrors** — regenerate per-agent mirrors from canonical `skills/`; confirm the
+   drift gate stays green (per AGENTS.md skill-sync learning). Update AGENTS.md docs index.
+9. **Behavioral verification** — run the superpowers-style acceptance test ("let's build X" →
+   `activation` routes to `/plan`, which orients + elicits + declares `/dev` as sole successor)
+   and record the transcript, mirroring superpowers' eval discipline.
+
+**Sequencing note:** Task 1 gates 2–6; Task 7 gates 8; Task 9 is the final acceptance. Tasks 3, 4,
+5 are per-skill fan-outs that can parallelize across worktrees once Task 1 lands.
+
+---
+
+## Open questions for team-lead
+- **Bootstrap name** — `activation` vs `flow` vs folding the Rule into existing `/status`? (Kept
+  `activation` to match the kernel issue title and keep `/status` a pure orient utility.)
+- **Digraph vs metadata-only** — adopt the full `dot` process-flow digraph in every skill, or just
+  the `next-skill:` key + prose? (Recommend metadata key always; digraph only where a skill
+  branches, e.g. `/plan --strategic`.)
+- **Scope of first PR** — ship the Contract spec + `activation` + `/plan` conversion as PR 1
+  (proves the pattern end-to-end), then fan out the remaining skills? (Recommended.)
